---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PasswordGenerator from '@/components/tools/security/PasswordGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="password-generator">
  <PasswordGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to generate a strong password</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Set the length. Drag the slider to at least 16 characters, or type an exact
        number in the box beside it. This is the single most important control on the page.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pick the character types. All four are on by default, giving a 94-character
        alphabet. Turn symbols off if the site you are signing up to rejects them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Adjust the exclusions if you need to. "Exclude similar characters" removes
        the glyphs people misread when copying a password off a screen; "exclude ambiguous
        punctuation" removes brackets and quotes that break shell scripts and CSV files.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check the meter. Aim for 80 bits or more. The number updates as you change
        the options, and the table underneath translates it into hours, years or geological epochs.
      </li>
      <li>
        Copy it and paste it straight into your password manager. The clipboard is
        overwritten again after 45 seconds so the password does not sit there for the rest of the
        day.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How long should a password be?</h2>
    <p>
      Length is the only property that keeps paying off. Every extra character multiplies the number
      of possibilities by the size of the alphabet, so going from 12 characters to 16 is worth
      vastly more than adding a second exclamation mark. The table below assumes a fully random
      password over the 94 printable ASCII characters, and an attacker who already has the password
      database and runs an eight-GPU rig against an unsalted fast hash. That is the worst realistic
      case.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Length</th>
          <th>Entropy</th>
          <th>Slow hash (bcrypt, 20k/s)</th>
          <th>Fast hash (8 GPUs, 100 G/s)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>52 bits</td><td>5 thousand years</td><td>8 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10</td><td>66 bits</td><td>43 million years</td><td>9 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12</td><td>79 bits</td><td>377 billion years</td><td>75 thousand years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>14</td><td>92 bits</td><td>beyond the age of the universe</td><td>666 million years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>16</td><td>105 bits</td><td>beyond the age of the universe</td><td>6 trillion years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>20</td><td>131 bits</td><td>beyond the age of the universe</td><td>beyond the age of the universe</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Eight characters fail fast against a fast hash. Eight characters was the standard advice for two
      decades, and it has been unsafe since about 2012. At 12 random characters, brute force is no
      longer the main risk. After that, the risk is phishing, malware and password reuse.
    </p>

    <h3>Alphabet size matters much less than you would think</h3>
    <p>
      A 16-character password drawn from letters and digits only (62 symbols) is worth 95 bits; the
      same length over the full 94-character set is 105 bits. Ten bits is real, but it is the same
      gain you get from adding two more characters to the shorter alphabet. So if a login form
      rejects symbols, make the password longer.
    </p>

    <h2>Why "must contain a symbol" rules backfire</h2>
    <p>
      Composition rules were designed to stop people choosing <code>password</code>. In practice they
      give <code>Password1!</code>: the same word with the same three additions as everyone else, in
      the same positions. Cracking tools have applied those exact
      transformations as standard rules since the 1990s. The rule adds perhaps two bits of real
      strength while making the password harder to remember, which pushes people towards reuse.
    </p>
    <p>
      This generator offers <em>include one of each type</em> because those rules exist and you have
      to satisfy them, not because they help. It is implemented so it costs you nothing measurable:
      instead of forcing a digit into a fixed position, the password is drawn uniformly from the set
      of strings that happen to satisfy the rule, and the entropy shown on the page is the exact
      entropy of that restricted set.
    </p>

    <h2>What this generator does differently</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No <code>Math.random</code>, anywhere.</strong> Every character comes from
        <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, the browser's cryptographically secure generator.
        <code>Math.random</code> is fast and predictable; given a few outputs its future values can
        be reconstructed.
      </li>
      <li>
        No modulo bias. Mapping a 32-bit random number onto a 94-character alphabet
        with a plain <code>%</code> makes the first few characters marginally more likely than the
        rest. This tool discards any draw that lands in the final partial bucket and takes another,
        so every character is exactly equally likely.
      </li>
      <li>
        Honest entropy. The figure shown is the entropy of the distribution the
        tool actually samples from, including the reduction caused by exclusions and the
        one-of-each rule.
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing is transmitted or persisted. No network requests after page load,
        nothing in local storage, nothing in the URL, nothing in your history.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What to do with the password once you have it</h2>
    <p>
      Put it in a password manager. Nobody can remember a generated 20-character password, and that is
      fine: you only need to remember one strong passphrase for the manager. The
      real threats to your accounts are, in order: reusing one password across sites, being phished
      into typing it on a lookalike page, and malware on your own machine. Brute force comes a
      distant fourth, and this page has already solved that one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Turn on two-factor authentication wherever it is offered. A leaked password with a second
      factor in place is an inconvenience; a leaked password without one is an account takeover.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
